"""Colour phosh's battery icon by how fast the battery is actually moving.

The phone shows a percentage and a lightning bolt, and both look the same
whether 1 watt or 6 are going in. That is the one number worth seeing at a
glance - a bad cable, a laptop port or a charger that has quietly renegotiated
all look like "charging" and cost hours.

So: green while it is going in fast, amber in the middle, red when it is
barely moving. Optionally the same on battery, where the meaning is reversed -
green is a phone that is drawing little.

How the colour gets there, because the obvious way does not work:

  phosh is GTK3 and its battery icon is the CSS node `phosh-battery-info`, so
  one line of CSS colours it. But GTK3 reads ~/.config/gtk-3.0/gtk.css exactly
  once, at program start, and never looks at it again - a colour that changes
  would need the shell restarted every time, which on this phone is the one
  thing not to do.

  What GTK3 does re-read at runtime is the THEME. Changing
  org.gnome.desktop.interface gtk-theme restyles every running GTK3 app,
  phosh included, within a second. So this writes three themes that are the
  user's own theme plus one line - `phosh-battery-info image { color: … }` -
  and switches between them by name.

That is also the cost, and it is worth knowing before turning this on: every
change restyles all GTK3 apps for a moment, and the theme setting shows one of
our names while it is active. It happens when the colour changes, not
continuously - a handful of times per charge.

Nothing here needs root. It reads two files in /sys, writes ~/.themes, and
sets one gsettings key.
"""

import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time

